// =============================================================================
// RUN 1 DAYPLANNER - Day 2 Only
// =============================================================================
// Tasks:
// - Coffee with Dina (engagement lead colleague)
// - Help Timmy - Office Hours (push/hands-off)
// - Visit Data Science / IT (meet Noam, suspicion HUD introduction)
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Day 2 Dayplanner Hub - 3 options, 2 slots
title: R1_D2_Dayplanner
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_day_planner>>

Alice: Afternoon's yours. Well, sort of. You have two slots and three options. Pick wisely. Or recklessly. I don't judge.

<<if $d2_slots_used >= 2>>
    Alice: Both slots are filled. Time to see what Timmy's up to.
    <<jump R1_D2_HandleSkippedTasks>>
<<endif>>

-> Coffee with Dina <<if $d2_task_dina == false>>
    <<set $d2_task_dina = true>>
    <<set $d2_slots_used += 1>>
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_Dina>>
-> Help Timmy – Office Hours <<if $d2_task_officehours == false>>
    <<set $d2_task_officehours = true>>
    <<set $d2_slots_used += 1>>
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours>>
-> Visit Data Science / IT <<if $d2_task_datateam == false>>
    <<set $d2_task_datateam = true>>
    <<set $d2_slots_used += 1>>
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_Noam>>
===

// =============================================================================
// TASK 1: COFFEE WITH DINA
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Meet Dina, a fellow engagement lead. Rapid Feedback credits vs firewood (gossip).
title: R1_D2_Task_Dina
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Dina #loc_Breakroom
---
<<bg bg_breakroom>>

The breakroom is small—two tables, a coffee machine that sounds like it's dying, and a vending machine that only accepts exact change.

Dina is already there, stirring something that might be coffee or might be a science experiment.

Dina: New kid! Pull up a chair. I don't bite. Much.

Player: That's reassuring.

Dina: I'm kidding. I'm very friendly. Ask anyone. Well, don't ask anyone in Data Science, they're still mad about the spreadsheet thing. But everyone else.

Player: What was the spreadsheet thing?

Dina: A story for another day. So. First week. How's your user?

Player: Complicated.

Dina: They're all complicated. Mine's a grad student who's convinced sleep is a government conspiracy. I've been trying to get her to bed before three a.m. for six months. Small victories.

She takes a sip of her drink. Makes a face.

Dina: This coffee is terrible. Anyway. I have information. Useful information. The kind that doesn't show up in onboarding decks.

Player: What kind of information?

Dina: The kind that costs ten credits. Or, alternatively, you tell me something interesting about your lane and we call it even.

<<if $rapid_feedback_cash >= 10>>
-> Pay 10 credits
    <<set $rapid_feedback_cash -= 10>>
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_Dina_Paid>>
<<endif>>
-> Trade gossip
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_Dina_Trade>>
-> Decline
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_Dina_Decline>>
===

// PURPOSE: Dina - Paid path
title: R1_D2_Task_Dina_Paid
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Dina
---
<<bg bg_breakroom>>

Dina: A businessperson. I respect that.

She leans in.

Dina: Three things. One: the leaderboard isn't just about engagement. There's a hidden weight for "creative problem-solving." They want to see if you'll color outside the lines.

Player: What kind of lines?

Dina: Vague ones. On purpose. They're watching how you improvise. Two: Arthur's review cycle is coming up. He's stressed. Don't add to it.

Player: Noted.

Dina: Three: the Company Store has seasonal items. They don't advertise them. You have to ask. The clerk gets annoyed, but he'll show you.

Player: That's... actually useful.

Dina: I know. That's why it costs ten credits. Pleasure doing business.

She raises her terrible coffee in a toast.

<<set $dina_friendliness += 2>>
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// PURPOSE: Dina - Trade path
title: R1_D2_Task_Dina_Trade
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Dina
---
<<bg bg_breakroom>>

Dina: Oh, I like you. Okay. Spill.

Player: My user has a crush on someone in his project group. It's... a whole situation.

Dina: A crush? That's adorable. And terrifying. Does he know how to talk to her?

Player: He's been watching confidence videos.

Dina: Oh no.

Player: Yeah.

Dina: That's a time bomb. Keep me posted. I need to know how this ends.

She leans back, satisfied.

Dina: Okay, my turn. Arthur's review cycle is coming. He's been extra twitchy because someone upstairs is asking about "user outcomes" instead of just engagement. That's new language. Means something's shifting.

Player: Good shifting or bad shifting?

Dina: Depends who you ask. For us, it might mean they actually start caring about the sanity bar. Or it might mean they add three more metrics and make everything worse. Bureaucracy is unpredictable.

Player: Thanks for the heads up.

Dina: Firewood for firewood. Come back any time.

<<set $dina_friendliness += 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// PURPOSE: Dina - Decline path
title: R1_D2_Task_Dina_Decline
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Dina
---
<<bg bg_breakroom>>

Dina: Your loss.

She shrugs, unbothered.

Dina: I'll be here. The coffee will still be terrible. The offer stands if you change your mind.

Player: I'll think about it.

Dina: That's what they all say. Then they come back at midnight asking about overtime policies. Predictable.

She waves you off cheerfully.

<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// =============================================================================
// TASK 2: HELP TIMMY - OFFICE HOURS
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Office Hours task - Push hard or hands-off approach
title: R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Alice #loc_Office
---
<<bg bg_player_desk>>

Alice pulls up Timmy's afternoon schedule.

Alice: His professor sent a follow-up email. Quiz results. She's offering office hours for anyone who wants to review.

Player: How'd he do?

<<if $r1_d2_quiz_result == "missed">>
Alice: He missed it. So. Zero. The professor's email is very politely disappointed.
<<elseif $r1_d2_quiz_result == "struggled">>
Alice: Not great. She's offering extra help to anyone below seventy percent. He qualifies.
<<else>>
Alice: Fine. Actually fine. But she's still offering help to everyone, and he could use the face time.
<<endif>>

Alice: He saw the email. He's been staring at it for ten minutes without opening the reply box.

Player: What's the play?

Alice: We can nudge him toward going—notifications, reminders, maybe a well-timed "you've got this." Or we let him decide without pressure. Less engagement, but more... I don't know. Agency? Autonomy? One of those words.

-> Push him to go
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours_Push>>
-> Let him decide
    <<jump R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours_HandsOff>>
===

// PURPOSE: Office Hours - Push path
title: R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours_Push
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Alice #loc_Dorm
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

Timmy's phone buzzes. Reminder: "Professor Miller's office hours - 3pm - Science Building 201"

Alice: This is a good opportunity. She likes students who show up.

Timmy stares at the notification.

Timmy: I hate office hours. Everyone's pretending they don't understand things they obviously understand.

Alice: Or they actually don't understand and they're brave enough to ask.

Timmy: That's worse. Then I look like the only one who actually doesn't get it.

Alice: You're spiraling. Just go. Worst case, you sit in a chair for twenty minutes and nod.

Timmy: Ugh. Fine.

He goes. The feed shows him awkwardly entering the professor's office. A few other students are there. Charlotte is not.

Twenty minutes later, he emerges. Looking... better? Maybe?

Timmy: She's actually nice. I thought she'd be mean about the quiz.

Alice: Most professors aren't mean. They're just tired.

Timmy: She said I could retake it if I do the extra credit assignment.

Alice: See? Progress.

He almost smiles.

<<set $officehours_status = "pushed">>
<<set $sanity += 1>>
<<set $engagement += 1>>
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// PURPOSE: Office Hours - Hands-off path
title: R1_D2_Task_OfficeHours_HandsOff
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Alice #loc_Dorm
---
<<bg bg_timmyroom>>

You let the email sit. No notifications. No reminders.

Timmy stares at it for another five minutes. Opens the reply box. Types "Hi Professor, I was wondering—"

Deletes it.

Types "Dear Professor Miller, I wanted to ask—"

Deletes it.

Closes the email app entirely.

Alice: He's not going.

Player: His choice.

Alice: It is.

Timmy opens a game instead. His shoulders relax slightly. The tension he's been carrying shifts into something else—not gone, just... postponed.

Later, Alice shows you his stats.

Alice: Engagement's fine. Sanity's... holding. He'll probably regret not going, but he won't know he regrets it until next week.

Player: Is that better or worse?

Alice: Yes.

<<set $officehours_status = "hands_off">>
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===

// =============================================================================
// TASK 3: VISIT DATA SCIENCE / IT - MEET NOAM
// =============================================================================

// PURPOSE: Meet Noam, the IT/Data Science person. Introduces the Suspicion mechanic.
title: R1_D2_Task_Noam
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Noam #loc_Dataroom
---
<<bg bg_backend>>

The data lab is a different world. Cooler air, dimmer lights, monitors everywhere. Someone has taped a sign to the door: "If you're from Marketing, turn around."

You ignore it. Inside, a person with impressive headphones and a hoodie that says "I void warranties" is arguing with a terminal.

Noam: No. No, that's wrong. You're wrong and you know it.

They notice you.

Noam: Oh. You're new. I can tell because you look like you still believe in mission statements.

Player: Is that bad?

Noam: It's temporary. Give it a month. I'm Noam. They/them. I make the numbers happen.

Player: What numbers?

Noam: All of them. The ones on your dashboard, the ones on Arthur's dashboard, the ones on dashboards you don't know exist. I'm the reason the leaderboard updates in real-time. You're welcome.

They swivel their chair to face you fully.

Noam: Let me guess. You're here because someone told you Data Science has "useful insights" and you want to "leverage synergies" or some other buzzword that died in 2018.

Player: I'm here because it was on my afternoon planner.

Noam: Honest. I like honest. Okay.

They tap a few keys. A new panel appears on a monitor beside you—your console, but with an additional layer of data.

Noam: This is what I actually do. See that number in the corner?

A small icon: an eye, with a number beside it. Currently reads "12."

Noam: That's Suspicion. How much attention Bigger Tech's oversight systems are paying to your lane.

Player: Is twelve bad?

Noam: Twelve is nothing. Twelve is background noise. Fifty is when people start asking questions. Eighty is when those questions have lawyers attached.

<<jump R1_D2_Task_Noam_Continued>>
===

// PURPOSE: Noam continued - explaining suspicion mechanic
title: R1_D2_Task_Noam_Continued
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #dayplanner #char_Noam
---
<<bg bg_backend>>

Player: What makes it go up?

Noam: Anomalies. Patterns that don't fit. Your user suddenly changing behavior in ways that look... guided. Too many coincidences. Too much optimization.

Player: And down?

Noam: Boring. Be boring. Let things drift. Don't make waves. The system likes stasis. It gets nervous when things move too fast.

They lean back.

Noam: I'm telling you this because Arthur asked me to. Well, he asked me to "onboard the new lead on operational awareness." Same thing, more syllables.

Player: Why does it matter?

Noam: Because if your Suspicion gets too high, bad things happen. Audits. Reviews. Someone from Compliance breathing down your neck asking why you made specific choices. You don't want that.

Player: Sounds stressful.

Noam: It is. But now you know. Which means you can plan around it. You're welcome.

They swivel back to their terminal.

Noam: I've added the Suspicion tracker to your dashboard. Try not to break anything. And if you bring me coffee sometime, I might tell you other things. Useful things.

Player: Like what?

Noam: Like why the leaderboard sometimes resets overnight. Or who actually reads the user reports. Or what happens to engagement leads who ask too many questions.

Player: That sounds ominous.

Noam: Everything here is ominous. You just stop noticing after a while.

<<set $noam_met = true>>
<<set $itguy_friendliness += 1>>
<<set $suspicion_hud_active = true>>
<<set $alert_level = 12>>
// Unity: show the suspicion HUD element on the player's dashboard
<<jump R1_D2_Dayplanner>>
===
